<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monte Cavo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mons Albanus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monte Cavo is the second-highest peak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, rising to about 950 metres above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albano">Lake Albano</a>. In antiquity, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> Albanus, it carried the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jupiter-latiaris">Jupiter Latiaris</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> peoples and later the Roman consuls celebrated the annual Latin festival. Roman tradition placed Alba Longa on the slopes nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monte-cavo.jpg" alt="Monte Cavo" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NikonZ7II · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Cavo_Visto_da_castel_gandolfo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Monte Cavo, less often called Monte Albano, is the dominant peak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a>. It is an old volcano that became extinct around 10,000 years ago, lying about 20 km from the sea in the territory of the comune of Rocca di Papa. Its present name comes from Cabum, an Italic settlement on the mountain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> reported volcanic activity there under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tullus-hostilius">Tullus Hostilius</a>, when there had been a shower of stones on the Alban Mount.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The mountain was sacred to the Albani of Alba Longa and other Italic peoples, and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, who built the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jupiter-latiaris">Jupiter Latiaris</a> there, one of the chief places of pilgrimage for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>. In 531 BCE King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a> built a temple there shared with the Latins, the Hernici and the Volsci. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feriae-latinae">Feriae Latinae</a> lasted four days and were attended by representatives of 47 cities; a consul who won a war also had to celebrate a triumph on the mount. A sacred way left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Appian Way</a> at Ariccia, and more than 5 km of it is preserved in the woods.</p><p>In the Early Middle Ages a Dalmatian hermit replaced the temple with a hermitage devoted to Saint Peter, visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> in 1463 and later by Pope Alexander VII. It became a monastery in 1727; the Passionists arrived in 1758 and restored it in 1783 with materials from the temple of Jupiter, found and raised by Henry Benedict Stuart, Duke of York and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frascati">Frascati</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> stayed there in 1865 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> in 1867.</p><p>The monks left in 1889, and from 1890 the building was a hotel whose guests included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luigi-pirandello">Luigi Pirandello</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armando-diaz">Armando Diaz</a> and the former King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a> with Wallis Simpson. From 1942 the German Wehrmacht used it as a base for radio communications, and on 3 June 1944 soldiers of the 142nd Regiment of the 36th Infantry Division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> captured the site, killing 20 enemy soldiers and taking 30 prisoners.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monte Cavo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeology shows Iron Age villages and cemeteries around the Alban Lake and <b>Monte Cavo</b> from about 1000 BCE, but no single city has been firmly identified.</blockquote><a href="/state/alba-longa">Alba Longa</a></li><li><blockquote>The Latin peoples of the plain south of the Tiber shared a language, the cult of Jupiter Latiaris on the <b>Alban Mount</b> and other common sanctuaries from the early Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/latin-league">Latin League</a></li></ul></section>
